|~ `::number` and what is declared under it: its constructor and the
   refusals of a payload of another kind and of an answer that leaves
   the doubles, and the words that run on a number. ~|

:number
  |~~ A number is a finite double, the one kind of number: a whole
      number and a decimal are one kind, `3 | eq 3.0` answering `true`,
      and a sum rounds as doubles round, `0.1 | add 0.2` answering
      `0.30000000000000004`. Infinity and NaN are no values, so a step
      whose answer leaves the range answers a failure.

      It is written as JSON writes it, `42`, `-1.5`, `2.5e-3`, and
      prints the shortest digits that read back, `1e21` printing
      `1e+21`; a literal past the range does not parse. `::number(5)` is
      `5`, while text is never read as a number by its look:
      `::number"5"` is refused, and `parseJson` reads a number out of a
      string, `"42" | parseJson | add 1` answering `43`. Numbers rank
      after booleans and before strings.

      Its words add, subtract, multiply, divide and compare, `add`,
      `sub`, `mul`, `div`, `mod`, `gt`, and round, `round`, `floor`,
      `ceil`; `sum`, `avg`, `min` and `max` read the numbers of a
      vector, and `range` counts up to one.
      ::snippet~(::number | manifest) lists its words and refusals.
      ::link~(::string/parseJson | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:number/isANumber
  |~~ Five is a number. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | type | eq ::number)

:number/refusesALiteralPastTheRange
  |~~ The literal 1e400 does not parse. ~~|
  ::law~("1e400" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)

:number/answersAnErrorPastTheRange
  |~~ A product past the range answers an error. ~~|
  ::law~(1e308 | mul 10 !| type | eq ::number/NotFiniteError)

:number/roundsAsADouble
  |~~ A tenth and two tenths add as doubles add. ~~|
  ::law~([-1 2.5e-3 (0.1 | add 0.2)] | eq [-1 0.0025 0.30000000000000004])

:number/construct
  |~~ Reads a number as itself, a finite double. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/PayloadNotNumberError
  |~~ `::number` reads its payload as the number it is; a payload of
      another kind is refused, and text is read by `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/PayloadNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string payload is refused; `parseJson` reads a number from text. ~~|
  ::law~(::number"5" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/PayloadNotNumberError ::string])

:number/NotFiniteError
  |~~ A number is a finite double: an answer brought under `::number`
      that left their range, a sum or a product too large, is refused
      here, whichever word computed it, and the trail names the step and
      the subject it ran on.
  ~~|

:number/NotFiniteError/refusesAProductPastTheRange
  |~~ A product past the range of doubles is refused at the step that made it. ~~|
  ::law~(1e308 | mul 10 | add 1 !| [type (/trail | map ~/step)]
    | eq [::number/NotFiniteError [~(mul 10)]])

:number/add
  |~~ Answers the subject plus the number the call gives, `10 | add 3`.
      The subject is a number: a vector adds to each of its elements by
      `map`, `map ~(add 1)`, and sums them by `sum`; a string joins by
      `append`; and text that holds a number is read first by
      `parseJson`, `"5" | parseJson | add 1`. The addend is computed
      against the subject, so `5 | add (mul 2)` adds ten, and two fields
      of one record add by `sumOf /x /y`. A number under a tag answers
      under the tag, `::Width(2) | add 3` answering `::Width(5)`, and a
      sum past the doubles is refused as no number.
      ::link~(::number/sub | doc) ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/add/answer
  |~~ The subject and the addend added, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::number

:number/add/addend
  |~~ The number added to the subject, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/add/addend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The addend is a number; text that holds one is read by `parseJson`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/add/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `add` adds to a number: two fields of one record add by
      `sumOf /x /y`, a vector adds to each element by `map`,
      `map ~(add 1)`, a string joins by `append`, and text that holds a
      number is read first by `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/add/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `add` adds one number to the subject: the numbers of a vector sum
      by `sum`, `[1 2 3] | sum`, and two fields of one record add by
      `sumOf /x /y`.
  ~~|

:number/add/addsTheNumber
  |~~ `add` answers the sum. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | add 3 | eq 13)

:number/add/computesTheAddendAgainstTheSubject
  |~~ The addend is computed against the subject. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | add (mul 2) | eq 15)

:number/add/refusesASumPastTheDoubles
  |~~ A sum past the doubles is refused as no number. ~~|
  ::law~(1e308 | add 1e308 !| type | eq ::number/NotFiniteError)

:number/add/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | add 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/add/addend/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ An addend that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | add "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/add/addend/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/add/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoNumbers
  |~~ Args of two numbers are refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | add 1 2 !| type | eq ::number/add/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:number/add/answersUnderTheTag
  |~~ A number under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Width(2) | add 3 | eq ::Width(5))

:number/sub
  |~~ Answers the subject, the minuend, less the number the call gives,
      `10 | sub 3`, which may fall below zero, `5 | sub 7` answering
      `-2`. The subject is a number: a vector subtracts from each of its
      elements by `map`, `map ~(sub 1)`, and text that holds a number is
      read first by `parseJson`. The subtrahend is computed against the
      subject, and two fields of one record subtract by
      `subOf /total /paid`. A number under a tag answers under the tag.
      ::link~(::number/add | doc) ::link~(::any/subOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/sub/answer
  |~~ The subject less the subtrahend, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::number

:number/sub/subtrahend
  |~~ The number subtracted from the subject, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The subtrahend is a number; text that holds one is read by
      `parseJson` first, `10 | sub ("3" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/sub/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `sub` subtracts from a number: two fields of one record subtract by
      `subOf /total /paid`, a vector subtracts from each element by
      `map`, `map ~(sub 1)`, and text that holds a number is read first by
      `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/sub/subtractsTheNumber
  |~~ `sub` answers the difference. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | sub 3 | eq 7)

:number/sub/fallsBelowZero
  |~~ A difference may fall below zero. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | sub 7 | eq -2)

:number/sub/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | sub 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/sub/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subtrahend that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | sub "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/sub/answersUnderTheTag
  |~~ A number under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Width(5) | sub 3 | eq ::Width(2))

:number/mul
  |~~ Answers the subject times the number the call gives, `10 | mul 3`,
      a fraction among them, `7 | mul 0.5` answering `3.5`. The subject
      is a number: a vector multiplies each of its elements by `map`,
      `map ~(mul 2)`, and text that holds a number is read first by
      `parseJson`. The multiplier is computed against the subject, so
      `5 | mul /` squares it, and two fields of one record multiply by
      `mulOf /price /qty`. A number under a tag answers under the tag,
      and a product past the doubles is refused as no number.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc) ::link~(::any/mulOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/mul/answer
  |~~ The subject times the multiplier, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::number

:number/mul/multiplier
  |~~ The number the subject is multiplied by, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError
  |~~ The multiplier is a number; text that holds one is read by
      `parseJson` first, `2 | mul ("3" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/mul/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `mul` multiplies a number: two fields of one record multiply by
      `mulOf /price /qty`, a vector multiplies each element by `map`,
      `map ~(mul 2)`, and text that holds a number is read first by
      `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/mul/multipliesByTheNumber
  |~~ `mul` answers the product. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | mul 3 | eq 30)

:number/mul/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | mul 2 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/mul/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A multiplier that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | mul "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/mul/answersUnderTheTag
  |~~ A number under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Width(2) | mul 3 | eq ::Width(6))

:number/mul/multipliesByAFraction
  |~~ A fraction multiplies. ~~|
  ::law~(7 | mul 0.5 | eq 3.5)

:number/mul/computesTheMultiplierAgainstTheSubject
  |~~ The multiplier is computed against the subject. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | mul / | eq 25)

:number/div
  |~~ Answers the subject, the dividend, divided by the number the call
      gives, a fraction where the division leaves one, `7 | div 2`
      answering `3.5`. A divisor of zero is refused, so a divisor that
      may be zero is tested first,
      `if (/count | eq 0) ~(0) ~(divOf /sum /count)`. The subject is a
      number: a vector divides each of its elements by `map`,
      `map ~(div 2)`. The divisor is computed against the subject, and
      two fields of one record divide by `divOf /sum /count`. A number
      under a tag answers under the tag.
      ::link~(::number/mul | doc) ::link~(::any/divOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/div/answer
  |~~ The subject divided by the divisor, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::number

:number/div/divisor
  |~~ The number the subject is divided by, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/div/divisor/NotNumberError
  |~~ The divisor is a number; text that holds one is read by `parseJson`
      first, `6 | div ("3" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/div/divisor/ZeroError
  |~~ The divisor is not zero: a division by zero has no finite answer,
      and a divisor that may be zero is tested first,
      `if (/count | eq 0) ~(0) ~(divOf /sum /count)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(ne 0)

:number/div/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `div` divides a number: two fields of one record divide by
      `divOf /sum /count`, a vector divides each element by `map`,
      `map ~(div 2)`, and text that holds a number is read first by
      `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/div/dividesByTheNumber
  |~~ `div` answers the quotient. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | div 2 | eq 5)

:number/div/leavesAFraction
  |~~ A division that leaves a fraction answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(7 | div 2 | eq 3.5)

:number/div/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | div 2 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/div/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/div/divisor/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A divisor that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | div "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/div/divisor/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/div/divisor/ZeroError/refusesZero
  |~~ A division by zero is refused under the divisor. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | div 0 !| type | eq ::number/div/divisor/ZeroError)

:number/div/answersUnderTheTag
  |~~ A number under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Width(6) | div 3 | eq ::Width(2))

:number/mod
  |~~ Answers the remainder of the subject divided by the number the
      call gives, its sign the divisor's: `7 | mod 3` answers `1` and
      `-7 | mod 3` answers `2`, so `mod 2` tells an even number from an
      odd one, and a fraction leaves its own, `7.5 | mod 2` answering
      `1.5`. The divisor is computed against the subject, and a number
      under a tag answers under the tag.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/mod/answer
  |~~ The remainder, its sign the divisor's, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::number

:number/mod/divisor
  |~~ The number the subject is divided by, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/mod/divisor/NotNumberError
  |~~ The divisor is a number; text that holds one is read by `parseJson`
      first, `7 | mod ("3" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/mod/divisor/ZeroError
  |~~ The divisor is not zero: a division by zero leaves no remainder,
      and a value that may be zero is asked first, `if (eq 0) …`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(ne 0)

:number/mod/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `mod` divides a number: text that holds one is read first by
      `parseJson`, `"7" | parseJson | mod 3`.
  ~~|

:number/mod/leavesTheRemainder
  |~~ `mod` answers what the division leaves. ~~|
  ::law~(7 | mod 3 | eq 1)

:number/mod/takesTheSignOfTheDivisor
  |~~ The remainder takes the sign of the divisor. ~~|
  ::law~([(-7 | mod 3) (7 | mod -3)] | eq [2 -2])

:number/mod/tellsAnEvenNumber
  |~~ `mod 2` answers zero for an even number. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4] | filter ~(mod 2 | eq 0) | eq [2 4])

:number/mod/leavesAFraction
  |~~ A fraction leaves a fraction. ~~|
  ::law~(7.5 | mod 2 | eq 1.5)

:number/mod/answersUnderTheTag
  |~~ A number under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Width(7) | mod 3 | eq ::Width(1))

:number/mod/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("7" | mod 3 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/mod/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/mod/divisor/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A divisor that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(7 | mod "3" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/mod/divisor/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/mod/divisor/ZeroError/refusesZero
  |~~ A remainder of a division by zero is refused under the divisor. ~~|
  ::law~(7 | mod 0 !| type | eq ::number/mod/divisor/ZeroError)

:number/abs
  |~~ Answers the subject without its sign, `-3 | abs` answering `3`, a
      number under a tag under the tag. It takes no value.
      ::link~(::number/sub | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/abs/answer
  |~~ The subject without its sign, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::number

:number/abs/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `abs` takes the sign off a number: text that holds one is read
      first by `parseJson`, `"-3" | parseJson | abs`.
  ~~|

:number/abs/dropsTheSign
  |~~ A negative number loses its sign, and a positive one stays. ~~|
  ::law~([-3 3 -0.5] | map ~abs | eq [3 3 0.5])

:number/abs/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("-3" | abs !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/abs/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/floor
  |~~ Answers the greatest whole number not above the subject,
      `2.7 | floor` answering `2` and `-2.1 | floor` answering `-3`;
      `round` answers the nearest and `ceil` the least not below. It
      takes no value.
      ::link~(::number/ceil | doc) ::link~(::number/round | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/floor/answer
  |~~ The greatest whole number not above the subject, under its tags. ~~|
  ::number

:number/floor/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `floor` rounds a number down: text that holds one is read first by
      `parseJson`, `"2.7" | parseJson | floor`.
  ~~|

:number/floor/roundsDown
  |~~ A number rounds down, a negative one away from zero. ~~|
  ::law~([2.7 -2.1 3] | map ~floor | eq [2 -3 3])

:number/floor/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("2.7" | floor !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/floor/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/ceil
  |~~ Answers the least whole number not below the subject, `2.1 | ceil`
      answering `3` and `-2.7 | ceil` answering `-2`; `floor` answers
      the greatest not above. It takes no value.
      ::link~(::number/floor | doc) ::link~(::number/round | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/ceil/answer
  |~~ The least whole number not below the subject, under its tags. ~~|
  ::number

:number/ceil/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `ceil` rounds a number up: text that holds one is read first by
      `parseJson`, `"2.1" | parseJson | ceil`.
  ~~|

:number/ceil/roundsUp
  |~~ A number rounds up, a negative one toward zero, and prints no negative zero. ~~|
  ::law~([2.1 -2.7 3 -0.5] | map ~ceil | eq [3 -2 3 0])

:number/ceil/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("2.1" | ceil !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/ceil/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/range
  |~~ Answers the whole numbers from zero up to the subject, the subject
      left out: `5 | range` answers `[0 1 2 3 4]`, and zero or below
      answers `[]`. A count from one is `range | map ~(add 1)`, and the
      vector reads as any other, `5 | range | map ~(mul 2)`. The subject
      is a whole number. It takes no value.
      ::link~(::vec/map | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/range/answer
  |~~ The whole numbers from zero below the subject. ~~|
  ::vec

:number/range/CountNotIntegerError
  |~~ The count of `range`, its subject, is a whole number: a fraction is
      rounded first, `7.5 | floor | range`.
  ~~|

:number/range/CountTooLargeError
  |~~ The count of `range` is no longer than the length a vector of the
      host may have, which `:longest` names: a count past it makes no
      vector, and the call has no repair but a count within it.
  ~~|

:number/range/CountTooLargeError/longest
  |~~ The length a vector of the host may have, the count past which `range` makes none. ~~|
  ::number

:number/range/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `range` counts up to a number: the count of a vector is read first,
      `[:a :b] | count | range`.
  ~~|

:number/range/countsFromZero
  |~~ The numbers start at zero and leave the subject out. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | range | eq [0 1 2 3 4])

:number/range/answersNothingBelowOne
  |~~ Zero and below answer the empty vector. ~~|
  ::law~([0 -2] | map ~range | eq [[] []])

:number/range/countsFromOneByMap
  |~~ A count from one adds one to each. ~~|
  ::law~(3 | range | map ~(add 1) | eq [1 2 3])

:number/range/CountNotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ A fraction is no count. ~~|
  ::law~(2.5 | range !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/range/CountNotIntegerError ::number])

:number/range/CountTooLargeError/refusesACountPastTheLongest
  |~~ A count past the length `:longest` names is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(1e10 | range !| [type (/longest | lt 1e10)] | eq [::number/range/CountTooLargeError true])

:number/range/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector is counted first. ~~|
  ::law~([:a :b] | range !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/range/SubjectNotNumberError ::vec])

:number/round
  |~~ Answers the subject rounded to the digits after the point the call
      gives, none when it gives none: `3.14159 | round 2` answers `3.14`
      and `2.5 | round` answers `3`. A half rounds away from zero, as
      the digits are written, `1.005 | round 2` answering `1.01` and
      `-2.5 | round` answering `-3`; a negative count rounds to tens and
      beyond, `1234 | round -2` answering `1200`; and a number rounded
      to zero prints as `0`. The digits are a whole number. The subject
      is a number, and text that holds one is read first by `parseJson`.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/round/answer
  |~~ The subject rounded to the digits. ~~|
  ::number

:number/round/digits
  |~~ The digits after the point, a whole number, none when the call
      gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0 0)

:number/round/digits/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count of digits is a number, `3.14159 | round 2`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError
  |~~ The digits of `round` are a whole number, `round 2`. ~~|

:number/round/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `round` rounds a number: text that holds one is read first by
      `parseJson`, `"2.5" | parseJson | round`.
  ~~|

:number/round/keepsTheDigitsGiven
  |~~ Two digits keep two places after the point. ~~|
  ::law~(3.14159 | round 2 | eq 3.14)

:number/round/roundsAHalfAwayFromZero
  |~~ A half rounds away from zero. ~~|
  ::law~(2.5 | round | eq 3)

:number/round/roundsANegativeHalfAwayFromZero
  |~~ A negative half rounds away from zero too. ~~|
  ::law~(-2.5 | round | eq -3)

:number/round/roundsTheDigitsAsWritten
  |~~ A half rounds as the digits are written. ~~|
  ::law~(1.005 | round 2 | eq 1.01)

:number/round/roundsToHundreds
  |~~ A negative count rounds to tens and beyond. ~~|
  ::law~(1234 | round -2 | eq 1200)

:number/round/printsNoNegativeZero
  |~~ A negative number rounded to zero prints as 0. ~~|
  ::law~(-0.4 | round | print | eq "0")

:number/round/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no number to round. ~~|
  ::law~("2.5" | round !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/round/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ Half a digit is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(2.5 | round 0.5 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError ::number])

:number/round/digits/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A count of digits that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(3.14 | round "2" !| type | eq ::number/round/digits/NotNumberError)

:number/gt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is greater than the number the call
      gives, `10 | gt 5`, a decimal against a whole number among them,
      `3 | gt 2.5`. Numbers compare with numbers: a string, a keyword, a
      tag name and a value under `::desc` each compare with their own
      kind by a `gt` of their own, and a vector, a set, a map or null
      answers no `gt`, which the refusal of the call names with the
      kinds that do. The value is computed against the subject, and in a
      condition the subject is the element, `filter ~(gt 1)`. `gte`
      answers whether the subject is greater or equal, `lt` and `lte`
      the reverse.
      ::link~(::number/gte | doc) ::link~(::number/lt | doc)
      ::link~(::number/lte | doc) ::link~(::desc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/gt/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/gt/value
  |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `5 | gt 3`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/gt/value/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number; text that holds one is read by
      `parseJson` first, `5 | gt ("3" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/gt/comparesNumbers
  |~~ Ten is greater than five. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | gt 5)

:number/gt/comparesADecimal
  |~~ A whole number compares with a decimal. ~~|
  ::law~(3 | gt 2.5)

:number/gt/keepsOneNumberApartFromItself
  |~~ A number is not greater than itself. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | gt 5 | not)

:number/gt/filtersByTheElement
  |~~ In a condition the subject is the element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1) | eq [2 3])

:number/gt/value/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | gt "a" !| type | eq ::number/gt/value/NotNumberError)

:number/gt/namesTheKindsThatAnswerIt
  |~~ A vector answers no `gt`, and the refusal names the kinds that do. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | gt [0] !| /addresses | eq #[::desc/gt ::keyword/gt ::number/gt ::string/gt ::tag/gt])

:number/gte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is greater than or equal to the number
      the call gives, `10 | gte 10`. It compares as `gt` does, numbers
      with numbers, a whole number with a decimal, `5 | gte 5.0`
      answering `true`.
      ::link~(::number/gt | doc) ::link~(::number/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/gte/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value or equals it, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/gte/value
  |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `5 | gte 3`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/gte/value/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number; text that holds one is read by
      `parseJson` first, `5 | gte ("3" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/gte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A number is greater than or equal to itself. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | gte 10)

:number/gte/holdsOfItsDecimal
  |~~ A whole number equals its decimal. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | gte 5.0)

:number/gte/value/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | gte "3" !| type | eq ::number/gte/value/NotNumberError)

:number/gte/failsBelow
  |~~ A smaller number is not at least the value. ~~|
  ::law~(2 | gte 3 | not)

:number/lt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is less than the number the call gives,
      `5 | lt 10`. It compares as `gt` does, numbers with numbers, and
      `lte` answers whether the subject is less or equal.
      ::link~(::number/gt | doc) ::link~(::number/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/lt/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/lt/value
  |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `5 | lt 3`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/lt/value/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number; text that holds one is read by
      `parseJson` first, `5 | lt ("3" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/lt/comparesNumbers
  |~~ Five is less than ten. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | lt 10)

:number/lt/keepsOneNumberApartFromItself
  |~~ A number is not less than itself. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | lt 5 | not)

:number/lt/value/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | lt "3" !| type | eq ::number/lt/value/NotNumberError)

:number/lte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is less than or equal to the number the
      call gives, `10 | lte 10`. It compares as `gt` does, numbers with
      numbers.
      ::link~(::number/lt | doc) ::link~(::number/gte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/lte/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value or equals it, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/lte/value
  |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `5 | lte 3`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/lte/value/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number; text that holds one is read by
      `parseJson` first, `5 | lte ("3" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/lte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A number is less than or equal to itself. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | lte 10)

:number/lte/value/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | lte "3" !| type | eq ::number/lte/value/NotNumberError)

:number/lte/failsAbove
  |~~ A greater number is not at most the value. ~~|
  ::law~(2 | lte 1 | not)
